## 2. Browser journeys, signed-in and anonymous, strict-parity switches at their defaults

- **What was measured:** 51 browser journeys on the synthetic deck, signed-in
  and anonymous (for example anonymous reading, search, sign-up and refused
  anonymous writes). One browser drives each journey against the TypeScript port
  and against the Ruby reference, and the checker compares completion, page
  addresses, text, structure, form fields, mail and database rows for each.
- **Date:** 2026-11-02 (run started 2026-11-02T18:42:54Z).
- **Source tree:** `f174eac26f17860d0c74f61dc8813aa425db91c0`. Every journey
  records the same clean source and the same build.
- **Runtime:** the port was served by Node v24.22.1 from its build, and one
  Chromium 155.0.8059.85 browser drove both sides. The run records do not name
  the operating system.
- **Configuration:** "default" in this section's title and in the other
  documents means only that the five `DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_*` switches were
  unset, so each took its default at this tree: the mail unfollow refusal on,
  the other four off. It does not mean the port ran with its out-of-the-box
  settings. The port side ran with the harness's port launcher
  (ts-server.mjs, which is not included in this package), without its
  `--production` option. At this tree (development
  commit be422339) the launcher set these settings for the port:
  - `DECKO_WRITE_PATH=1`: the write path on, so journeys can save cards.
  - `DECKO_MAIL_CAPTURE=1`: outgoing mail captured in memory so the checker
    can compare it, instead of being dropped.
  - `DECKO_FILES_DIR` and `COMPILED_ASSETS_DIR`: both the files directory of
    the session's disposable copy of the synthetic deck.
  - `DECKO_ORIGIN`: the launcher's own loopback address,
    `http://127.0.0.1:<port>`. The renderer's deck origin was set to the same
    address in code, and the fixture's clock and default page view were
    applied in code.
  - `DECKO_TOKEN_SECRET`: a fixed test secret.
  - `DECKO_SIGNIN_UNIFORM_FAILURE=off`, `DECKO_SIGNIN_THROTTLE=off` and
    `DECKO_SESSION_REVOCATION=off`, so the port answers sign-ins as the Ruby
    reference does.
  - `DECKO_PASSWORD_HASH=legacy-sha1`.
  - `DECKO_FONT_AWESOME=local` (the vendored same-origin Font Awesome), unless
    already set.
  - `DECKO_DECK_PROFILE=stock`: the deck is served as stock Decko serves it.
  - `DECKO_ASSET_REFRESH=never`, unless already set: compiled script and style
    outputs are never rewritten, as the Ruby reference is configured.
  - `DECKO_MOD_PUBLIC_DIR`: the reference application's public directory
    (not included in this package; section 5 of reference-harness/SPEC.md
    describes the reference application), unless the run named a Decko
    checkout, in which case that checkout. The run's records do not say which.

  It left these unset, so they took their defaults:
  - **Serializer era:** `DECKO_SERIALIZER_ERA` not set, so `stock`, the
    default. The reasons this still matched the live Ruby 3.2.3 reference are
